---
title: Multi-Turn Structured Chat
id: structured-outputs-multi-turn
order: 4
description: "Build a chat where users iterate on a typed object across multiple turns — each successfully completed structured-output run adds a typed response to message history, and messages[i].parts.find(p => p.type === 'structured-output') is typed by your schema."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - structured outputs
  - multi-turn
  - structured chat
  - StructuredOutputPart
  - typed message parts
  - UIMessage
  - recipe builder
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want users to iterate on a structured object across turns. \"Give me a pasta recipe under $15\" → recipe card lands. \"Now make it vegan\" → a new recipe card lands; the old one stays visible in history. \"Add a salad and make it gluten-free\" → third card lands; the first two are still there to compare against."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the shape of a structured-output chat: each successfully completed structured-output run adds a structured-output assistant message, every old response stays renderable, and the type of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages[i].parts.find(p => p.type === 'structured-output').data"},{"type":"text","value":" is your schema's inferred type — not "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By the end of this guide you'll have a chat UI that walks "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" directly, renders one typed card per successfully completed structured-output run, and keeps history across "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendMessage()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" If you only need a single round-trip (one prompt → one object), use "},{"type":"link","href":"./one-shot","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One-Shot Extraction"}]},{"type":"text","value":". If you have one turn that streams progressively but no history, use "},{"type":"link","href":"./streaming","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming UIs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" sugar is the right surface. This page is for the case where history matters."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Native recipe app:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The Expo example streams this same multi-turn\nrecipe pattern into native cards with an XHR transport selector. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../getting-started/quick-start-react-native","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick Start: React Native"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to\nrun it on Expo Go."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-it-lands-on-the-message","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How it lands on the message"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":" receives the server's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" event, the runtime attaches a typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MessagePart"},{"type":"text","value":" to the assistant "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UIMessage"},{"type":"text","value":" carrying the structured response. The part looks like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { DeepPartial } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype StructuredOutputPart<TData> = {\n  type: \"structured-output\";\n  status: \"streaming\" | \"complete\" | \"error\";\n  /** Progressive parse of `raw` — populated while streaming and after complete. */\n  partial?: DeepPartial<TData>;\n  /** Completed typed object — set when `status === \"complete\"`. */\n  data?: TData;\n  /** Accumulating JSON text. Round-trip source of truth for the next turn. */\n  raw: string;\n  /** Optional reasoning tokens surfaced by thinking models. */\n  reasoning?: string;\n  /** Set when `status === \"error\"`. */\n  errorMessage?: string;\n};","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TData"},{"type":"text","value":" flows from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat({ outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":" through the framework-package message types ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UIMessage<TTools, TData>"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MessagePart<TTools, TData>"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-client"},{"type":"text","value":", which the React / Vue / Solid / Svelte hooks re-export) down to the structured-output variant. The result: when you call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat({ outputSchema: RecipeSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages[i].parts.find(p => p.type === \"structured-output\")"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputPart<Recipe>"},{"type":"text","value":" — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" is typed as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Recipe"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DeepPartial<Recipe>"},{"type":"text","value":". No manual cast."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The core "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai"},{"type":"text","value":" package defines "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MessagePart<TData>"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UIMessage<TData>"},{"type":"text","value":" with a single generic (no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TTools"},{"type":"text","value":") — the tools generic lives in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-client"},{"type":"text","value":" and the framework hook packages. If you're building UI, you almost always want to import from your framework package ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-react"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-vue"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-solid"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-svelte"},{"type":"text","value":") or from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-client"},{"type":"text","value":" — those carry both generics. The core types come into play only if you're working at the adapter layer below the client."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each new structured response lands on a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"new"}]},{"type":"text","value":" assistant message with its "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"own"}]},{"type":"text","value":" structured-output part. Earlier responses stay untouched. That's what makes \"show history\" trivial."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A run may also contain assistant messages without a structured-output part. Native-combined output keeps the structured JSON and its part on one assistant message. On the separate-finalization path, a plain-text assistant message can precede the structured-output assistant message. Find the part by type instead of assuming that every assistant message contains one."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-endpoint","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server endpoint"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The server side is the same as a single-turn streaming endpoint. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })"},{"type":"text","value":" happens to be multi-turn safe out of the box because the server sees the full conversation history on every request and emits one structured-output run per request."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// app/api/structured-chat/route.ts\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nexport const RecipeSchema = z.object({\n  title: z.string(),\n  cuisine: z.string(),\n  servings: z.number(),\n  estimatedCostUsd: z.number(),\n  ingredients: z.array(\n    z.object({ item: z.string(), amount: z.string() }),\n  ),\n  steps: z.array(z.string()),\n  tips: z.array(z.string()),\n});\n\nexport type Recipe = z.infer<typeof RecipeSchema>;\n\nconst SYSTEM_PROMPT = `You are a chef assistant. Always respond with a single recipe matching the JSON schema. When the user asks for modifications, produce a new recipe in the same shape that reflects the change.`;\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    systemPrompts: [SYSTEM_PROMPT],\n    outputSchema: RecipeSchema,\n    stream: true,\n  });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behind the scenes, when the client sends turn N, prior "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UIMessage.parts"},{"type":"text","value":" remain intact in the request. The client also mirrors each completed structured-output part's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"raw"},{"type":"text","value":" JSON into assistant content. Server conversion preserves the structured-output marker while adapters consume the provider-facing content. Multi-turn coherence is preserved without you doing anything special."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-walk-the-messages","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client: walk the messages"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's the shape you want. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" exposes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" (typed, schema-aware), "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendMessage"},{"type":"text","value":", and the hook-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" sugar for the latest turn. To render history, walk "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" directly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useState } from \"react\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport type { StructuredOutputPart } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { RecipeSchema, type Recipe } from \"./api/structured-chat\";\nimport { UserBubble } from \"./components\";\n\n// The schema-typed structured-output part. Pulled out so the find()\n// predicate below stays readable.\ntype RecipePart = StructuredOutputPart<Recipe>;\n\nfunction StructuredChatPage() {\n  const [input, setInput] = useState(\"\");\n\n  const { messages, sendMessage, isLoading } = useChat({\n    outputSchema: RecipeSchema,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/structured-chat\"),\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((m) => {\n        if (m.role === \"user\") {\n          const text = m.parts\n            .filter((p) => p.type === \"text\")\n            .map((p) => p.content)\n            .join(\"\");\n          return <UserBubble key={m.id} text={text} />;\n        }\n        if (m.role === \"assistant\") {\n          // `data` is typed as `Recipe` because the schema generic flows\n          // all the way from useChat through messages[i].parts.\n          const recipePart = m.parts.find(\n            (p): p is RecipePart => p.type === \"structured-output\",\n          );\n          if (!recipePart) return null;\n          return <RecipeCard key={m.id} part={recipePart} />;\n        }\n        return null;\n      })}\n\n      <input\n        value={input}\n        onChange={(e) => setInput(e.target.value)}\n        disabled={isLoading}\n      />\n      <button\n        onClick={() => {\n          sendMessage(input);\n          setInput(\"\");\n        }}\n        disabled={isLoading}\n      >\n        Send\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nfunction RecipeCard({ part }: { part: RecipePart }) {\n  // After you check `part.status === \"complete\"`, TypeScript knows that\n  // `part.data` is a `Recipe`. No `!` or `?.` is necessary.\n  // While the model streams the JSON, `partial` is `DeepPartial<Recipe>`.\n  const recipe = part.status === \"complete\" ? part.data : part.partial;\n\n  return (\n    <article>\n      <h3>{recipe?.title ?? \"Plating up…\"}</h3>\n      {recipe?.cuisine && <p>{recipe?.cuisine}</p>}\n      {recipe?.ingredients?.map((ing, i) => (\n        <li key={i}>\n          {ing?.amount} {ing?.item}\n        </li>\n      ))}\n      {part.status === \"error\" && (\n        <p>Failed: {part.errorMessage ?? \"Stream failed\"}</p>\n      )}\n    </article>\n  );\n}","lang":"tsx","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That's it. The render loop above produces a card per structured response. When the user sends a follow-up, a new structured-output assistant message arrives — the old card stays exactly as it was."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the full pattern in code:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the example app at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"examples/ts-react-chat/src/routes/generations.structured-chat.tsx"},{"type":"text","value":" ships a polished version of this exact recipe-builder UI — empty state, streaming placeholder, cuisine-aware hero banner, ingredients grid, numbered method, chef's tips block. Use it as a reference for visual layout; the data wiring matches what's shown above."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"streaming-the-latest-turn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming the latest turn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output"},{"type":"text","value":" part normally goes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"streaming"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"complete"},{"type":"text","value":" (or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"streaming"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":"). A terminal-only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"complete"},{"type":"text","value":" event can also arrive with no prior streaming deltas. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" field only populates on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"complete"},{"type":"text","value":". While the model is still emitting JSON, only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"raw"},{"type":"text","value":" are filled in. Render against "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.data ?? part.partial"},{"type":"text","value":" and the UI fills in field by field as bytes arrive, then snaps to the completed typed object on the terminal event."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hook-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" are still available. They're derived from the most recent structured-output part after the latest user message — the same part the render loop above already finds. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendMessage()"},{"type":"text","value":" and the first chunk (because no structured-output part exists yet to derive from), and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" until the latest turn lands its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"complete"},{"type":"text","value":" event. Use them for sticky-summary widgets (\"Latest recipe title: …\"); use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" walk for the full history view."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-safe-access-without-a-named-alias","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type-safe access without a named alias"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The example above pulls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type RecipePart = StructuredOutputPart<Recipe>"},{"type":"text","value":" out for readability. If you'd rather not name it, you can narrow inline with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Extract"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { RecipeSchema, type Recipe } from \"./api/structured-chat\";\n\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  outputSchema: RecipeSchema,\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/structured-chat\"),\n});\n\nfor (const m of messages) {\n  const recipePart = m.parts.find(\n    (p): p is Extract<typeof p, { type: \"structured-output\" }> =>\n      p.type === \"structured-output\",\n  );\n  // `recipePart` is `StructuredOutputPart<Recipe> | undefined`.\n  // `recipePart.data` is `Recipe | undefined`.\n}","lang":"tsx","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both forms produce the same typed result. Pick whichever you find more readable."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-about-the-round-trip","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What about the round-trip?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When turn N+1 fires, completed structured-output parts remain on their UI messages and are mirrored into provider-facing assistant content using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.raw"},{"type":"text","value":". Streaming and errored parts remain UI state but are excluded from model input."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"raw"},{"type":"text","value":" is empty (rare — a terminal-only complete event arrived before any deltas, then the runtime couldn't serialize the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" either), the part remains in UI state but is excluded from provider-facing content. This avoids sending a blank assistant turn to the model."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Combining with tools?"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Multi-turn structured chats compose with the agent loop the same way single-turn streams do — each turn runs tools first, then produces a structured-output message. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./with-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for tool-approval gating and client-tool invocations inside a structured-chat run."}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"how-it-lands-on-the-message","text":"How it lands on the message","level":2},{"id":"server-endpoint","text":"Server endpoint","level":2},{"id":"client-walk-the-messages","text":"Client: walk the messages","level":2},{"id":"streaming-the-latest-turn","text":"Streaming the latest turn","level":2},{"id":"type-safe-access-without-a-named-alias","text":"Type-safe access without a named alias","level":2},{"id":"what-about-the-round-trip","text":"What about the round-trip?","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou want users to iterate on a structured object across turns. \"Give me a pasta recipe under $15\" → recipe card lands. \"Now make it vegan\" → a new recipe card lands; the old one stays visible in history. \"Add a salad and make it gluten-free\" → third card lands; the first two are still there to compare against.\n\nThis is the shape of a structured-output chat: each successfully completed structured-output run adds a structured-output assistant message, every old response stays renderable, and the type of `messages[i].parts.find(p => p.type === 'structured-output').data` is your schema's inferred type — not `unknown`.\n\nBy the end of this guide you'll have a chat UI that walks `messages` directly, renders one typed card per successfully completed structured-output run, and keeps history across `sendMessage()` calls.\n\n> **Note:** If you only need a single round-trip (one prompt → one object), use [One-Shot Extraction](./one-shot). If you have one turn that streams progressively but no history, use [Streaming UIs](./streaming) — its `partial` / `final` sugar is the right surface. This page is for the case where history matters.\n\n> **React Native recipe app:** The Expo example streams this same multi-turn\n> recipe pattern into native cards with an XHR transport selector. See\n> [Quick Start: React Native](../getting-started/quick-start-react-native) to\n> run it on Expo Go.\n\n## How it lands on the message\n\nWhen `useChat({ outputSchema })` receives the server's `structured-output.complete` event, the runtime attaches a typed `structured-output` `MessagePart` to the assistant `UIMessage` carrying the structured response. The part looks like this:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { DeepPartial } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype StructuredOutputPart<TData> = {\n  type: \"structured-output\";\n  status: \"streaming\" | \"complete\" | \"error\";\n  /** Progressive parse of `raw` — populated while streaming and after complete. */\n  partial?: DeepPartial<TData>;\n  /** Completed typed object — set when `status === \"complete\"`. */\n  data?: TData;\n  /** Accumulating JSON text. Round-trip source of truth for the next turn. */\n  raw: string;\n  /** Optional reasoning tokens surfaced by thinking models. */\n  reasoning?: string;\n  /** Set when `status === \"error\"`. */\n  errorMessage?: string;\n};\n```\n\n`TData` flows from `useChat({ outputSchema })` through the framework-package message types (`UIMessage<TTools, TData>` and `MessagePart<TTools, TData>` in `@tanstack/ai-client`, which the React / Vue / Solid / Svelte hooks re-export) down to the structured-output variant. The result: when you call `useChat({ outputSchema: RecipeSchema })`, `messages[i].parts.find(p => p.type === \"structured-output\")` returns `StructuredOutputPart<Recipe>` — `data` is typed as `Recipe`, `partial` as `DeepPartial<Recipe>`. No manual cast.\n\n> **Note:** The core `@tanstack/ai` package defines `MessagePart<TData>` and `UIMessage<TData>` with a single generic (no `TTools`) — the tools generic lives in `@tanstack/ai-client` and the framework hook packages. If you're building UI, you almost always want to import from your framework package (`@tanstack/ai-react` / `-vue` / `-solid` / `-svelte`) or from `@tanstack/ai-client` — those carry both generics. The core types come into play only if you're working at the adapter layer below the client.\n\nEach new structured response lands on a **new** assistant message with its **own** structured-output part. Earlier responses stay untouched. That's what makes \"show history\" trivial.\n\nA run may also contain assistant messages without a structured-output part. Native-combined output keeps the structured JSON and its part on one assistant message. On the separate-finalization path, a plain-text assistant message can precede the structured-output assistant message. Find the part by type instead of assuming that every assistant message contains one.\n\n## Server endpoint\n\nThe server side is the same as a single-turn streaming endpoint. `chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })` happens to be multi-turn safe out of the box because the server sees the full conversation history on every request and emits one structured-output run per request.\n\n```typescript\n// app/api/structured-chat/route.ts\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nexport const RecipeSchema = z.object({\n  title: z.string(),\n  cuisine: z.string(),\n  servings: z.number(),\n  estimatedCostUsd: z.number(),\n  ingredients: z.array(\n    z.object({ item: z.string(), amount: z.string() }),\n  ),\n  steps: z.array(z.string()),\n  tips: z.array(z.string()),\n});\n\nexport type Recipe = z.infer<typeof RecipeSchema>;\n\nconst SYSTEM_PROMPT = `You are a chef assistant. Always respond with a single recipe matching the JSON schema. When the user asks for modifications, produce a new recipe in the same shape that reflects the change.`;\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    systemPrompts: [SYSTEM_PROMPT],\n    outputSchema: RecipeSchema,\n    stream: true,\n  });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\nBehind the scenes, when the client sends turn N, prior `UIMessage.parts` remain intact in the request. The client also mirrors each completed structured-output part's `raw` JSON into assistant content. Server conversion preserves the structured-output marker while adapters consume the provider-facing content. Multi-turn coherence is preserved without you doing anything special.\n\n## Client: walk the messages\n\nHere's the shape you want. `useChat` exposes `messages` (typed, schema-aware), `sendMessage`, and the hook-level `partial` / `final` sugar for the latest turn. To render history, walk `messages` directly:\n\n```tsx ignore\nimport { useState } from \"react\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport type { StructuredOutputPart } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { RecipeSchema, type Recipe } from \"./api/structured-chat\";\nimport { UserBubble } from \"./components\";\n\n// The schema-typed structured-output part. Pulled out so the find()\n// predicate below stays readable.\ntype RecipePart = StructuredOutputPart<Recipe>;\n\nfunction StructuredChatPage() {\n  const [input, setInput] = useState(\"\");\n\n  const { messages, sendMessage, isLoading } = useChat({\n    outputSchema: RecipeSchema,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/structured-chat\"),\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((m) => {\n        if (m.role === \"user\") {\n          const text = m.parts\n            .filter((p) => p.type === \"text\")\n            .map((p) => p.content)\n            .join(\"\");\n          return <UserBubble key={m.id} text={text} />;\n        }\n        if (m.role === \"assistant\") {\n          // `data` is typed as `Recipe` because the schema generic flows\n          // all the way from useChat through messages[i].parts.\n          const recipePart = m.parts.find(\n            (p): p is RecipePart => p.type === \"structured-output\",\n          );\n          if (!recipePart) return null;\n          return <RecipeCard key={m.id} part={recipePart} />;\n        }\n        return null;\n      })}\n\n      <input\n        value={input}\n        onChange={(e) => setInput(e.target.value)}\n        disabled={isLoading}\n      />\n      <button\n        onClick={() => {\n          sendMessage(input);\n          setInput(\"\");\n        }}\n        disabled={isLoading}\n      >\n        Send\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nfunction RecipeCard({ part }: { part: RecipePart }) {\n  // After you check `part.status === \"complete\"`, TypeScript knows that\n  // `part.data` is a `Recipe`. No `!` or `?.` is necessary.\n  // While the model streams the JSON, `partial` is `DeepPartial<Recipe>`.\n  const recipe = part.status === \"complete\" ? part.data : part.partial;\n\n  return (\n    <article>\n      <h3>{recipe?.title ?? \"Plating up…\"}</h3>\n      {recipe?.cuisine && <p>{recipe?.cuisine}</p>}\n      {recipe?.ingredients?.map((ing, i) => (\n        <li key={i}>\n          {ing?.amount} {ing?.item}\n        </li>\n      ))}\n      {part.status === \"error\" && (\n        <p>Failed: {part.errorMessage ?? \"Stream failed\"}</p>\n      )}\n    </article>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nThat's it. The render loop above produces a card per structured response. When the user sends a follow-up, a new structured-output assistant message arrives — the old card stays exactly as it was.\n\n> **See the full pattern in code:** the example app at `examples/ts-react-chat/src/routes/generations.structured-chat.tsx` ships a polished version of this exact recipe-builder UI — empty state, streaming placeholder, cuisine-aware hero banner, ingredients grid, numbered method, chef's tips block. Use it as a reference for visual layout; the data wiring matches what's shown above.\n\n## Streaming the latest turn\n\nA `structured-output` part normally goes `streaming` → `complete` (or `streaming` → `error`). A terminal-only `complete` event can also arrive with no prior streaming deltas. The `data` field only populates on `complete`. While the model is still emitting JSON, only `partial` and `raw` are filled in. Render against `part.data ?? part.partial` and the UI fills in field by field as bytes arrive, then snaps to the completed typed object on the terminal event.\n\nThe hook-level `partial` and `final` are still available. They're derived from the most recent structured-output part after the latest user message — the same part the render loop above already finds. `partial` returns `{}` between `sendMessage()` and the first chunk (because no structured-output part exists yet to derive from), and `final` returns `null` until the latest turn lands its `complete` event. Use them for sticky-summary widgets (\"Latest recipe title: …\"); use the `messages` walk for the full history view.\n\n## Type-safe access without a named alias\n\nThe example above pulls `type RecipePart = StructuredOutputPart<Recipe>` out for readability. If you'd rather not name it, you can narrow inline with `Extract`:\n\n```tsx ignore\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { RecipeSchema, type Recipe } from \"./api/structured-chat\";\n\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  outputSchema: RecipeSchema,\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/structured-chat\"),\n});\n\nfor (const m of messages) {\n  const recipePart = m.parts.find(\n    (p): p is Extract<typeof p, { type: \"structured-output\" }> =>\n      p.type === \"structured-output\",\n  );\n  // `recipePart` is `StructuredOutputPart<Recipe> | undefined`.\n  // `recipePart.data` is `Recipe | undefined`.\n}\n```\n\nBoth forms produce the same typed result. Pick whichever you find more readable.\n\n## What about the round-trip?\n\nWhen turn N+1 fires, completed structured-output parts remain on their UI messages and are mirrored into provider-facing assistant content using `part.raw`. Streaming and errored parts remain UI state but are excluded from model input.\n\nIf `raw` is empty (rare — a terminal-only complete event arrived before any deltas, then the runtime couldn't serialize the `data` either), the part remains in UI state but is excluded from provider-facing content. This avoids sending a blank assistant turn to the model.\n\n> **Combining with tools?** Multi-turn structured chats compose with the agent loop the same way single-turn streams do — each turn runs tools first, then produces a structured-output message. See [With Tools](./with-tools) for tool-approval gating and client-tool invocations inside a structured-chat run.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Multi-Turn Structured Chat\nid: structured-outputs-multi-turn\norder: 4\ndescription: \"Build a chat where users iterate on a typed object across multiple turns — each successfully completed structured-output run adds a typed response to message history, and messages[i].parts.find(p => p.type === 'structured-output') is typed by your schema.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - structured outputs\n  - multi-turn\n  - structured chat\n  - StructuredOutputPart\n  - typed message parts\n  - UIMessage\n  - recipe builder\n---\n\nYou want users to iterate on a structured object across turns. \"Give me a pasta recipe under $15\" → recipe card lands. \"Now make it vegan\" → a new recipe card lands; the old one stays visible in history. \"Add a salad and make it gluten-free\" → third card lands; the first two are still there to compare against.\n\nThis is the shape of a structured-output chat: each successfully completed structured-output run adds a structured-output assistant message, every old response stays renderable, and the type of `messages[i].parts.find(p => p.type === 'structured-output').data` is your schema's inferred type — not `unknown`.\n\nBy the end of this guide you'll have a chat UI that walks `messages` directly, renders one typed card per successfully completed structured-output run, and keeps history across `sendMessage()` calls.\n\n> **Note:** If you only need a single round-trip (one prompt → one object), use [One-Shot Extraction](./one-shot). If you have one turn that streams progressively but no history, use [Streaming UIs](./streaming) — its `partial` / `final` sugar is the right surface. This page is for the case where history matters.\n\n> **React Native recipe app:** The Expo example streams this same multi-turn\n> recipe pattern into native cards with an XHR transport selector. See\n> [Quick Start: React Native](../getting-started/quick-start-react-native) to\n> run it on Expo Go.\n\n## How it lands on the message\n\nWhen `useChat({ outputSchema })` receives the server's `structured-output.complete` event, the runtime attaches a typed `structured-output` `MessagePart` to the assistant `UIMessage` carrying the structured response. The part looks like this:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { DeepPartial } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype StructuredOutputPart<TData> = {\n  type: \"structured-output\";\n  status: \"streaming\" | \"complete\" | \"error\";\n  /** Progressive parse of `raw` — populated while streaming and after complete. */\n  partial?: DeepPartial<TData>;\n  /** Completed typed object — set when `status === \"complete\"`. */\n  data?: TData;\n  /** Accumulating JSON text. Round-trip source of truth for the next turn. */\n  raw: string;\n  /** Optional reasoning tokens surfaced by thinking models. */\n  reasoning?: string;\n  /** Set when `status === \"error\"`. */\n  errorMessage?: string;\n};\n```\n\n`TData` flows from `useChat({ outputSchema })` through the framework-package message types (`UIMessage<TTools, TData>` and `MessagePart<TTools, TData>` in `@tanstack/ai-client`, which the React / Vue / Solid / Svelte hooks re-export) down to the structured-output variant. The result: when you call `useChat({ outputSchema: RecipeSchema })`, `messages[i].parts.find(p => p.type === \"structured-output\")` returns `StructuredOutputPart<Recipe>` — `data` is typed as `Recipe`, `partial` as `DeepPartial<Recipe>`. No manual cast.\n\n> **Note:** The core `@tanstack/ai` package defines `MessagePart<TData>` and `UIMessage<TData>` with a single generic (no `TTools`) — the tools generic lives in `@tanstack/ai-client` and the framework hook packages. If you're building UI, you almost always want to import from your framework package (`@tanstack/ai-react` / `-vue` / `-solid` / `-svelte`) or from `@tanstack/ai-client` — those carry both generics. The core types come into play only if you're working at the adapter layer below the client.\n\nEach new structured response lands on a **new** assistant message with its **own** structured-output part. Earlier responses stay untouched. That's what makes \"show history\" trivial.\n\nA run may also contain assistant messages without a structured-output part. Native-combined output keeps the structured JSON and its part on one assistant message. On the separate-finalization path, a plain-text assistant message can precede the structured-output assistant message. Find the part by type instead of assuming that every assistant message contains one.\n\n## Server endpoint\n\nThe server side is the same as a single-turn streaming endpoint. `chat({ outputSchema, stream: true })` happens to be multi-turn safe out of the box because the server sees the full conversation history on every request and emits one structured-output run per request.\n\n```typescript\n// app/api/structured-chat/route.ts\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nexport const RecipeSchema = z.object({\n  title: z.string(),\n  cuisine: z.string(),\n  servings: z.number(),\n  estimatedCostUsd: z.number(),\n  ingredients: z.array(\n    z.object({ item: z.string(), amount: z.string() }),\n  ),\n  steps: z.array(z.string()),\n  tips: z.array(z.string()),\n});\n\nexport type Recipe = z.infer<typeof RecipeSchema>;\n\nconst SYSTEM_PROMPT = `You are a chef assistant. Always respond with a single recipe matching the JSON schema. When the user asks for modifications, produce a new recipe in the same shape that reflects the change.`;\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    systemPrompts: [SYSTEM_PROMPT],\n    outputSchema: RecipeSchema,\n    stream: true,\n  });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\nBehind the scenes, when the client sends turn N, prior `UIMessage.parts` remain intact in the request. The client also mirrors each completed structured-output part's `raw` JSON into assistant content. Server conversion preserves the structured-output marker while adapters consume the provider-facing content. Multi-turn coherence is preserved without you doing anything special.\n\n## Client: walk the messages\n\nHere's the shape you want. `useChat` exposes `messages` (typed, schema-aware), `sendMessage`, and the hook-level `partial` / `final` sugar for the latest turn. To render history, walk `messages` directly:\n\n```tsx ignore\nimport { useState } from \"react\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport type { StructuredOutputPart } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { RecipeSchema, type Recipe } from \"./api/structured-chat\";\nimport { UserBubble } from \"./components\";\n\n// The schema-typed structured-output part. Pulled out so the find()\n// predicate below stays readable.\ntype RecipePart = StructuredOutputPart<Recipe>;\n\nfunction StructuredChatPage() {\n  const [input, setInput] = useState(\"\");\n\n  const { messages, sendMessage, isLoading } = useChat({\n    outputSchema: RecipeSchema,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/structured-chat\"),\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((m) => {\n        if (m.role === \"user\") {\n          const text = m.parts\n            .filter((p) => p.type === \"text\")\n            .map((p) => p.content)\n            .join(\"\");\n          return <UserBubble key={m.id} text={text} />;\n        }\n        if (m.role === \"assistant\") {\n          // `data` is typed as `Recipe` because the schema generic flows\n          // all the way from useChat through messages[i].parts.\n          const recipePart = m.parts.find(\n            (p): p is RecipePart => p.type === \"structured-output\",\n          );\n          if (!recipePart) return null;\n          return <RecipeCard key={m.id} part={recipePart} />;\n        }\n        return null;\n      })}\n\n      <input\n        value={input}\n        onChange={(e) => setInput(e.target.value)}\n        disabled={isLoading}\n      />\n      <button\n        onClick={() => {\n          sendMessage(input);\n          setInput(\"\");\n        }}\n        disabled={isLoading}\n      >\n        Send\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nfunction RecipeCard({ part }: { part: RecipePart }) {\n  // After you check `part.status === \"complete\"`, TypeScript knows that\n  // `part.data` is a `Recipe`. No `!` or `?.` is necessary.\n  // While the model streams the JSON, `partial` is `DeepPartial<Recipe>`.\n  const recipe = part.status === \"complete\" ? part.data : part.partial;\n\n  return (\n    <article>\n      <h3>{recipe?.title ?? \"Plating up…\"}</h3>\n      {recipe?.cuisine && <p>{recipe?.cuisine}</p>}\n      {recipe?.ingredients?.map((ing, i) => (\n        <li key={i}>\n          {ing?.amount} {ing?.item}\n        </li>\n      ))}\n      {part.status === \"error\" && (\n        <p>Failed: {part.errorMessage ?? \"Stream failed\"}</p>\n      )}\n    </article>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nThat's it. The render loop above produces a card per structured response. When the user sends a follow-up, a new structured-output assistant message arrives — the old card stays exactly as it was.\n\n> **See the full pattern in code:** the example app at `examples/ts-react-chat/src/routes/generations.structured-chat.tsx` ships a polished version of this exact recipe-builder UI — empty state, streaming placeholder, cuisine-aware hero banner, ingredients grid, numbered method, chef's tips block. Use it as a reference for visual layout; the data wiring matches what's shown above.\n\n## Streaming the latest turn\n\nA `structured-output` part normally goes `streaming` → `complete` (or `streaming` → `error`). A terminal-only `complete` event can also arrive with no prior streaming deltas. The `data` field only populates on `complete`. While the model is still emitting JSON, only `partial` and `raw` are filled in. Render against `part.data ?? part.partial` and the UI fills in field by field as bytes arrive, then snaps to the completed typed object on the terminal event.\n\nThe hook-level `partial` and `final` are still available. They're derived from the most recent structured-output part after the latest user message — the same part the render loop above already finds. `partial` returns `{}` between `sendMessage()` and the first chunk (because no structured-output part exists yet to derive from), and `final` returns `null` until the latest turn lands its `complete` event. Use them for sticky-summary widgets (\"Latest recipe title: …\"); use the `messages` walk for the full history view.\n\n## Type-safe access without a named alias\n\nThe example above pulls `type RecipePart = StructuredOutputPart<Recipe>` out for readability. If you'd rather not name it, you can narrow inline with `Extract`:\n\n```tsx ignore\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { RecipeSchema, type Recipe } from \"./api/structured-chat\";\n\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  outputSchema: RecipeSchema,\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/structured-chat\"),\n});\n\nfor (const m of messages) {\n  const recipePart = m.parts.find(\n    (p): p is Extract<typeof p, { type: \"structured-output\" }> =>\n      p.type === \"structured-output\",\n  );\n  // `recipePart` is `StructuredOutputPart<Recipe> | undefined`.\n  // `recipePart.data` is `Recipe | undefined`.\n}\n```\n\nBoth forms produce the same typed result. Pick whichever you find more readable.\n\n## What about the round-trip?\n\nWhen turn N+1 fires, completed structured-output parts remain on their UI messages and are mirrored into provider-facing assistant content using `part.raw`. Streaming and errored parts remain UI state but are excluded from model input.\n\nIf `raw` is empty (rare — a terminal-only complete event arrived before any deltas, then the runtime couldn't serialize the `data` either), the part remains in UI state but is excluded from provider-facing content. This avoids sending a blank assistant turn to the model.\n\n> **Combining with tools?** Multi-turn structured chats compose with the agent loop the same way single-turn streams do — each turn runs tools first, then produces a structured-output message. See [With Tools](./with-tools) for tool-approval gating and client-tool invocations inside a structured-chat run.\n"}
